WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.637
Validation tests values against documented rules.

00:00:03.637 --> 00:00:08.582
A rule might describe a text pattern, a length, a domain, or a numeric range.

00:00:08.582 --> 00:00:11.922
Passing a rule means the value satisfies that rule.

00:00:11.922 --> 00:00:15.852
It does not necessarily prove the value is correct in the real world.

00:00:15.852 --> 00:00:19.697
Keep the distinction between validity and accuracy from Module 1.

00:00:19.697 --> 00:00:24.962
Connect this idea to a customer master-data project cleaning duplicate profiles.

00:00:24.962 --> 00:00:30.027
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.027 --> 00:00:37.007
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.007 --> 00:00:42.292
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:47.680
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
